.nh
.TH "PVEFORGE" "1" "Sep 2026" "pveforge" "pveforge manual"

.SH NAME
pveforge-access-keys - List pveforge's SSH keys in root's authorized_keys, and which roster holds each (read-only)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpveforge access keys <target-id> [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
List every key in root's authorized_keys on a target whose comment begins
pveforge@, read as root over SSH, and say which roster target holds each: a
target whose [targets.ssh] public_key is exactly that line. Nothing is
written, and nothing is removed.

.PP
On PVE, root's authorized_keys is /etc/pve/priv/authorized_keys, shared by
every node of the cluster: the list is the cluster's, whichever node is read.

.PP
A key no roster checked holds is a "stray", listed with remove_command: the
exact command that removes that one line when run as root on the node. It
removes the line only if it appears exactly once, writes nothing if the
file changes while it runs, and writes the new file beside the resolved
target (on PVE in /etc/pve/priv) and renames it onto it, so the symlink
~/.ssh/authorized_keys is never replaced and the cluster's file never
unshared. A stray whose line holds a carriage return (a CRLF file) gets no
command (remove_command_withheld says why): remove it by hand. A stray is
typically left by a first bootstrap that failed after installing its key,
on a pveforge older than the one that removes its own key again. pveforge
never removes a stray itself.

.PP
A key is held when a target's public_key is the same key, whatever options
(from="...", no-pty) or comment the line in the file carries.

.PP
The rosters checked are --roster (the one the target is read from) and each
--also-roster. A key that another roster uses, one not checked (a
colleague's, one on another machine, a test roster), ALSO reads as a stray:
check every roster that can reach this cluster before removing anything.
Lines without a pveforge@ comment are never listed.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--also-roster\fP=[]
	another roster whose SSH keys count as held (repeatable): every roster that can reach this cluster, so a key one of them uses is not reported as a stray

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for keys

.PP
\fB--host-key-fingerprint\fP=""
	the target's SSH host key fingerprint as you verified it, SHA256:<base64> exactly as ssh-keygen -l -E sha256 prints it: for a target holding no pin, the --no-ssh-key password connection must present that key, checked before the password is sent, instead of trusting the host key on first use; for a target holding a pin it must equal that pin, or nothing is dialed. It must be the fingerprint of the key type pveforge's SSH client negotiates: ECDSA when the host serves one (PVE does by default), so pinning the host's ED25519 key is refused there (fail-closed); ssh-keyscan and ssh-keygen -lf list every type the host serves.

.PP
\fB--no-ssh-key\fP[=false]
	connect as root with the PVE password (PVEFORGE_PVE_PASSWORD, else a prompt) for this run only, as bootstrap --no-ssh-key does; the connection is checked against the target's stored host key pin, else --host-key-fingerprint, else the host key is trusted on first use; required for a target that holds no SSH key

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP="kv"
	output format: "kv" or "json"

.PP
\fB--roster\fP=""
	path to the roster file (overrides PVEFORGE_ROSTER and the default ./pveforge.toml)


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpveforge-access(1)\fP
